# Implement a Multi-Producer, Multi-Consumer (MPMC) Queue in C++

For a MAANG Staff Engineer interview, we'll extend the previous MPSC queue to support multiple producer threads and multiple consumer threads, 
using `std::mutex` and `std::condition_variable`.

The implementation should guarantee:

- Multiple producers can safely push items concurrently.
- Multiple consumers can safely retrieve items concurrently.
- Consumers sleep when the queue is empty instead of busy-waiting.
- Producers can wait when the queue reaches its capacity.
- Shutdown wakes all waiting threads and allows queued items to be processed before consumers exit.

We'll implement a bounded blocking queue in C++17. Bounded capacity is useful in production systems because it prevents 
the queue from growing indefinitely when producers outpace consumers.

## 1. Complete implementation

```

#include <condition_variable>
#include <iostream>
#include <mutex>
#include <queue>
#include <thread>
#include <vector>

class MPMCQueue {
private:
    std::queue<int> q;
    const std::size_t capacity;

    std::mutex mtx;
    std::condition_variable not_empty;
    std::condition_variable not_full;

    bool stopped = false;

public:
    explicit MPMCQueue(std::size_t max_capacity)
        : capacity(max_capacity) {
        if (capacity == 0) {
            throw std::invalid_argument("Capacity must be > 0");
        }
    }

    // Multiple producers can call push().
    bool push(int value) {
        std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lock(mtx);

        not_full.wait(lock, [this] {
            return q.size() < capacity || stopped;
        });

        if (stopped) {
            return false;
        }

        q.push(value);

        lock.unlock();
        not_empty.notify_one();

        return true;
    }

    // Multiple consumers can call pop().
    bool pop(int& value) {
        std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lock(mtx);

        not_empty.wait(lock, [this] {
            return !q.empty() || stopped;
        });

        // Exit only after shutdown and queue drainage.
        if (q.empty()) {
            return false;
        }

        value = q.front();
        q.pop();

        lock.unlock();
        not_full.notify_one();

        return true;
    }

    // Stop accepting new items and wake all blocked threads.
    void shutdown() {
        {
            std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(mtx);
            stopped = true;
        }

        not_empty.notify_all();
        not_full.notify_all();
    }
};

int main() {
    constexpr int NUM_PRODUCERS = 3;
    constexpr int NUM_CONSUMERS = 2;
    constexpr int ITEMS_PER_PRODUCER = 5;

    MPMCQueue queue(4); // Bounded capacity = 4

    std::vector<std::thread> producers;
    std::vector<std::thread> consumers;

    // Start multiple consumers.
    for (int c = 0; c < NUM_CONSUMERS; ++c) {
        consumers.emplace_back([c, &queue] {
            int value;

            while (queue.pop(value)) {
                std::cout << "Consumer " << c
                          << " processed " << value << '\n';
            }

            std::cout << "Consumer " << c
                      << " exiting\n";
        });
    }

    // Start multiple producers.
    for (int p = 0; p < NUM_PRODUCERS; ++p) {
        producers.emplace_back([p, &queue] {
            for (int i = 0; i < ITEMS_PER_PRODUCER; ++i) {
                int value = p * 100 + i;

                if (queue.push(value)) {
                    std::cout << "Producer " << p
                              << " pushed " << value << '\n';
                }
            }
        });
    }

    // Wait until every producer has finished.
    for (auto& producer : producers) {
        producer.join();
    }

    // No more items will be produced.
    queue.shutdown();

    // Consumers drain remaining items and exit.
    for (auto& consumer : consumers) {
        consumer.join();
    }

    std::cout << "All threads completed\n";
    return 0;
}

```

Compile and run:

```
g++ -std=c++17 -pthread mpmc_queue.cpp -o mpmc_queue
./mpmc_queue
```

The output order is nondeterministic because the threads run concurrently. Every successfully pushed item is removed by exactly one consumer in this example.

## 2. How the two condition variables work

The key difference from the previous MPSC implementation is that we use two condition variables.

`not_empty` — consumer coordination

Consumers wait when the queue is empty. A producer calls `not_empty.notify_one()` after inserting an item.

`not_full` — producer coordination

Producers wait when the queue reaches its capacity. A consumer calls `not_full.notify_one()` after removing an item.

## 3. Producer-side implementation

```
bool push(int value) {
    std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lock(mtx);

    not_full.wait(lock, [this] {
        return q.size() < capacity || stopped;
    });

    if (stopped) {
        return false;
    }

    q.push(value);

    lock.unlock();
    not_empty.notify_one();

    return true;
}
```

The sequence is:

1. Acquire the mutex.
2. Wait if the queue is full.
3. Recheck the predicate when awakened.
4. Reject the operation if shutdown has started.
5. Insert the item while holding the mutex.
6. Release the mutex and notify one waiting consumer.

Why check the predicate again? Multiple producers might wake together, but only one may get the newly available slot. The others must wait again if the queue is full.

## 4. Consumer-side implementation

```
bool pop(int& value) {
    std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lock(mtx);

    not_empty.wait(lock, [this] {
        return !q.empty() || stopped;
    });

    if (q.empty()) {
        return false;
    }

    value = q.front();
    q.pop();

    lock.unlock();
    not_full.notify_one();

    return true;
}
```

Multiple consumers can wake concurrently, but the mutex ensures that only one consumer at a time removes an item.

Notice that `stopped` does not immediately force consumers to exit. If the queue still contains items, consumers continue draining it. 
A consumer returns `false` only when the queue is empty and shutdown has started.

## 5. Shutdown and thread-safety considerations

```
void shutdown() {
    {
        std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(mtx);
        stopped = true;
    }

    not_empty.notify_all();
    not_full.notify_all();
}
```

We use `notify_all()` during shutdown because every blocked producer and consumer must have the opportunity to wake up and exit.

The calling code must join all producers before invoking `shutdown()` if it intends to process every item they produce. Otherwise, a producer may have its push rejected after shutdown.

One additional improvement for production code is to decide how exceptions during item construction, allocation, or processing should be handled. 
This implementation uses `int`, so it keeps the example focused on concurrency.

## 6. Staff-level interview comparison

| Property              | MPSC           | MPMC                                     |
| --------------------- | -------------- | ---------------------------------------- |
| Producers             | Multiple       | Multiple                                 |
| Consumers             | One            | Multiple                                 |
| Queue mutex           | Required       | Required                                 |
| Consumer notification | `notify_one()` | `notify_one()`                           |
| Producer backpressure | Optional       | Optional; implemented here               |
| Shutdown wakeup       | Wake consumer  | Wake all blocked producers and consumers |

Interview takeaway: The central challenge in MPMC is not just protecting the queue. It is coordinating both sides of a bounded buffer, 
preserving invariants under contention, and ensuring shutdown cannot strand blocked threads.
